WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.100 --> 00:00:02.010
- This is my friend, Dr.Heather Irobunda.

00:00:02.010 --> 00:00:04.300
She dances on TikTok,
she's an army veteran,

00:00:04.300 --> 00:00:06.780
and she's a board certified OB/GYN

00:00:06.780 --> 00:00:07.870
right here in New York.

00:00:07.870 --> 00:00:10.810
Basically, she's better than
I am in a lot of things.

00:00:10.810 --> 00:00:12.236
Especially the dancing.

00:00:12.236 --> 00:00:14.420
(shoes skidding)

00:00:14.420 --> 00:00:15.550
Today, I wanted to take a look

00:00:15.550 --> 00:00:17.880
at a really controversial
episode of "The Good Doctor,"

00:00:17.880 --> 00:00:20.570
so I called my good friend
to get her perspective.

00:00:20.570 --> 00:00:22.720
And boy, I'm glad I did.

00:00:22.720 --> 00:00:24.758
These shows with the central lines

00:00:24.758 --> 00:00:25.591
(Heather laughs)

00:00:25.591 --> 00:00:26.877
Put a midline in, bro!

00:00:26.877 --> 00:00:28.380
(Mike makes a comical sound)

00:00:28.380 --> 00:00:30.020
- [Female Doctor] You have
two rambunctious boys.

00:00:30.020 --> 00:00:32.410
- I know, it's like a cage
match in here. (laughs)

00:00:32.410 --> 00:00:33.680
- Shaun, where's your first year resident?

00:00:33.680 --> 00:00:35.240
- I have two first year residents,

00:00:35.240 --> 00:00:36.700
but I don't want to teach them.

00:00:36.700 --> 00:00:38.040
They're very distracting.

00:00:38.040 --> 00:00:41.887
You have a dizygotic dichorionic
diamniotic pregnancy.

00:00:41.887 --> 00:00:43.810
(record scratch)
- How does he know that?

00:00:43.810 --> 00:00:44.680
- He's a magician.

00:00:44.680 --> 00:00:47.277
That's the thing, these shows
don't make any sense to me.

00:00:47.277 --> 00:00:48.180
- 'Cause like (laughs).

00:00:48.180 --> 00:00:50.640
Number one, he's a general surgery.

00:00:50.640 --> 00:00:52.100
Number two, he was like,

00:00:52.100 --> 00:00:53.300
how far away?

00:00:53.300 --> 00:00:55.805
And was like, "Those are (laughing)

00:00:55.805 --> 00:00:56.940
diamniotic dizygotic twins."

00:00:56.940 --> 00:00:58.700
- That he saw all of them.

00:00:58.700 --> 00:00:59.533
- Yeah, like-

00:00:59.533 --> 00:01:01.560
- All those parts without scanning.

00:01:01.560 --> 00:01:02.410
- Without scanning,

00:01:02.410 --> 00:01:04.130
without looking around,
- Not checking pockets.

00:01:04.130 --> 00:01:05.353
What if it was a bicornuate uterus?

00:01:05.353 --> 00:01:06.186
(bell dings)

00:01:06.186 --> 00:01:07.019
- [Dr. Irobunda] What?

00:01:07.019 --> 00:01:08.340
- [Dr. Mike] Look at that!
- [Dr. Irobunda] Look at that.

00:01:08.340 --> 00:01:10.162
- Family medicine, hitting
them with medical terms.

00:01:10.162 --> 00:01:10.995
(Dr. Irobunda makes popping noise)

00:01:10.995 --> 00:01:12.020
- Honestly, to know

00:01:12.020 --> 00:01:15.290
if it's like diamniotic, dizygotic,

00:01:15.290 --> 00:01:17.520
and depending on how far along she is,

00:01:17.520 --> 00:01:18.353
it may be hard.

00:01:18.353 --> 00:01:19.730
Her belly is pretty big,

00:01:19.730 --> 00:01:22.670
so it's harder sometimes
to know those things,

00:01:22.670 --> 00:01:24.460
unless you are able to see

00:01:24.460 --> 00:01:25.970
what we call the chorion,

00:01:25.970 --> 00:01:28.610
which is the separation
between the two sacs.

00:01:28.610 --> 00:01:29.690
- She has edema.

00:01:29.690 --> 00:01:30.690
- Not surprising for someone

00:01:30.690 --> 00:01:32.210
who's 26 weeks pregnant.

00:01:32.210 --> 00:01:33.800
- Pretty big belly for 26 weeks.

00:01:33.800 --> 00:01:35.112
- Yes.
- Would you agree?

00:01:35.112 --> 00:01:37.044
- Already did that.

00:01:37.044 --> 00:01:38.173
(machine beeps and hums)

00:01:38.173 --> 00:01:39.530
- What was her BP?

00:01:39.530 --> 00:01:40.970
- Is he going to
preeclampsia (indistinct)?

00:01:40.970 --> 00:01:43.200
- I feel like he's hinting to it,

00:01:43.200 --> 00:01:44.033
'cause whenever,

00:01:44.033 --> 00:01:45.200
I feel like any of these shows,

00:01:45.200 --> 00:01:46.287
if they say edema, they're like-

00:01:46.287 --> 00:01:47.793
- Edema and he's rushing
for the blood pressure pump.

00:01:47.793 --> 00:01:50.020
- They're like, "What's
your blood pressure like?"

00:01:50.020 --> 00:01:52.100
Honestly, in terms of preeclampsia,

00:01:52.100 --> 00:01:55.640
that is not actually one of
the criteria to diagnose it.

00:01:55.640 --> 00:01:56.631
It used to be.

00:01:56.631 --> 00:01:57.464
- Visual.

00:01:57.464 --> 00:02:00.340
- Spots in the vision, blurry
vision, high blood pressure.

00:02:00.340 --> 00:02:03.030
So 140 over 90 or higher.

00:02:03.030 --> 00:02:06.600
Headaches that don't go away
with Tylenol or acetaminophen.

00:02:06.600 --> 00:02:08.310
If you have pain in your
right upper quadrant,

00:02:08.310 --> 00:02:09.550
so by your liver.

00:02:09.550 --> 00:02:12.060
There's also protein that
you'll see in the urine.

00:02:12.060 --> 00:02:14.920
We have to check your urine
in order to know that, so.

00:02:14.920 --> 00:02:16.230
- But he's going to know
it magically, right now.

00:02:16.230 --> 00:02:18.540
- I know he's going to
be like, preeclampsia.

00:02:18.540 --> 00:02:21.330
- BP is no longer normal.
She's hypertensive.

00:02:21.330 --> 00:02:23.303
- What does that mean, Dr. Murray?

00:02:26.775 --> 00:02:28.000
- Is your vision blurred?

00:02:28.000 --> 00:02:29.293
- A bit, I'm tired.

00:02:30.570 --> 00:02:31.773
- It sounds like preeclampsia.

00:02:31.773 --> 00:02:33.259
(Dr. Mike laughs)

00:02:33.259 --> 00:02:34.092
- It's always-
- We got it.

00:02:34.092 --> 00:02:35.850
That was a teamwork.
- It's always preeclampsia.

00:02:35.850 --> 00:02:36.683
(Dr. Irobunda laughs)

00:02:36.683 --> 00:02:38.700
- We need to start you
on antihypertensive meds,

00:02:38.700 --> 00:02:40.980
but your edema makes it
difficult to place an IV,

00:02:40.980 --> 00:02:43.500
so we need to run a central line.

00:02:43.500 --> 00:02:46.210
- These shows with the central lines.

00:02:46.210 --> 00:02:47.973
Put a midline in, bro!
(Dr. Irobunda laughs)

00:02:47.973 --> 00:02:51.090
- Oh man, that got really intense

00:02:51.090 --> 00:02:52.090
really quickly.

00:02:52.090 --> 00:02:54.360
- Since when do we expose patients

00:02:54.360 --> 00:02:56.050
to jugular vein lines?
- Exactly.

00:02:56.050 --> 00:02:57.700
- Exactly, because do you know

00:02:57.700 --> 00:03:00.567
how much we're exposing to bacteria and-

00:03:00.567 --> 00:03:02.120
- And they make mistakes.

00:03:02.120 --> 00:03:04.990
- And you can bleed out.
- You can cut the wrong thing

00:03:04.990 --> 00:03:06.321
and cut a carotid.

00:03:06.321 --> 00:03:07.510
- Oh good God.

00:03:07.510 --> 00:03:08.860
And they were talking about

00:03:09.980 --> 00:03:11.860
giving her antihypertensives

00:03:11.860 --> 00:03:12.693
and I'm like.

00:03:12.693 --> 00:03:13.810
I mean, you can do that,

00:03:13.810 --> 00:03:16.120
but you can also give
some oral medications

00:03:16.120 --> 00:03:16.953
for it too.
- Right?

00:03:16.953 --> 00:03:17.786
- And it depends on

00:03:17.786 --> 00:03:19.294
how hypertensive she is.
- Hypertensive she is.

00:03:19.294 --> 00:03:21.350
- And I don't think the number
was like through the roof.

00:03:21.350 --> 00:03:22.200
I mean, I didn't see it really.

00:03:22.200 --> 00:03:23.033
- I mean I didn't see it,

00:03:23.033 --> 00:03:24.869
but I don't feel like they showed us.

00:03:24.869 --> 00:03:25.810
- We'll see.

00:03:25.810 --> 00:03:28.260
- And he has an apple and
you know what apples do.

00:03:28.260 --> 00:03:29.093
- Dr. Jackson?

00:03:29.093 --> 00:03:31.040
- Keep doctors away.
- Keep doctors away.

00:03:31.040 --> 00:03:32.990
And why is he eating during this time?

00:03:32.990 --> 00:03:35.390
- And also why is he
eating in a patient's room.

00:03:36.570 --> 00:03:38.400
- Fetal heart rates dropping.

00:03:38.400 --> 00:03:41.510
- That's not how a fetal
heart rate would usually look

00:03:41.510 --> 00:03:42.710
if it was dropping.

00:03:42.710 --> 00:03:44.000
- I just want to say that.

00:03:44.000 --> 00:03:44.833
It was just like--

00:03:44.833 --> 00:03:45.666
- I've never seen it that way either.

00:03:45.666 --> 00:03:46.970
- Yeah, if it was dropping,

00:03:46.970 --> 00:03:49.570
you would actually see it dropping.

00:03:49.570 --> 00:03:51.810
I feel like those were
not, you know, heart rates.

00:03:51.810 --> 00:03:52.643
That...

00:03:52.643 --> 00:03:54.110
- Yeah, they don't look legit.

00:03:54.110 --> 00:03:56.790
- Yeah. And baby's heart
rates are way faster.

00:03:56.790 --> 00:03:59.050
- Did you have another birth?

00:03:59.050 --> 00:04:00.710
- No.
- Any pregnancy?

00:04:00.710 --> 00:04:01.543
Miscarriage?

00:04:01.543 --> 00:04:02.987
Abortion, stillbirth?.

00:04:03.990 --> 00:04:05.770
- Had an abortion when I was 15.

00:04:05.770 --> 00:04:07.865
- ABO.
- Stop preeclampsia.

00:04:07.865 --> 00:04:08.698
- ABO.

00:04:08.698 --> 00:04:10.350
Fetus is RH positive.

00:04:10.350 --> 00:04:11.986
- It's not (Dr. Irobunda laughs).

00:04:11.986 --> 00:04:12.821
- I knew it.

00:04:12.821 --> 00:04:14.860
RH incompatibility.

00:04:14.860 --> 00:04:16.900
- With antibodies, antibodies.

00:04:16.900 --> 00:04:19.683
She could only have, if she
had an earlier pregnancy.

00:04:20.800 --> 00:04:23.370
- My body is trying to
kill one of my babies?

00:04:23.370 --> 00:04:24.230
- Wow.
- Don't they check this?

00:04:24.230 --> 00:04:25.720
- Like, 26 Weeks,

00:04:25.720 --> 00:04:27.010
we are definitely checking.

00:04:27.010 --> 00:04:29.560
So part of the initial prenatal visit,

00:04:29.560 --> 00:04:30.900
we do a whole bunch of lab work.

00:04:30.900 --> 00:04:33.830
So we're checking to see if
there's any infectious disease,

00:04:33.830 --> 00:04:35.470
you know, to make sure you're not anemic.

00:04:35.470 --> 00:04:38.240
And we also check to see the RH factor.

00:04:38.240 --> 00:04:39.810
So RH positive or negative

00:04:39.810 --> 00:04:41.630
because we're concerned
about things like this,

00:04:41.630 --> 00:04:45.750
where if you are RH negative
and you do have antibodies,

00:04:45.750 --> 00:04:48.120
we can start monitoring way sooner

00:04:48.120 --> 00:04:49.660
than 26 weeks for this.

00:04:49.660 --> 00:04:51.770
- The only time that this could happen

00:04:51.770 --> 00:04:54.010
is if she wasn't getting prenatal care,

00:04:54.010 --> 00:04:54.910
which does happen.

00:04:54.910 --> 00:04:56.450
- Which does happen, yeah.

00:04:56.450 --> 00:04:58.090
And that's why we push patients

00:04:58.090 --> 00:04:59.090
to go earlier

00:04:59.090 --> 00:05:00.460
and start the process earlier.

00:05:00.460 --> 00:05:01.931
So we can be proactive.

00:05:01.931 --> 00:05:04.709
- Dr. Jackson, what's our next step?

00:05:04.709 --> 00:05:05.542
- Oh.

00:05:05.542 --> 00:05:06.375
- Oh, what's happening?

00:05:06.375 --> 00:05:07.208
- What's going on here

00:05:07.208 --> 00:05:09.070
is this pregnancy has twin to twin

00:05:09.070 --> 00:05:10.600
Transfusion Syndrome.

00:05:10.600 --> 00:05:12.280
They have the same placenta

00:05:12.280 --> 00:05:14.440
and they're sharing blood flow.

00:05:14.440 --> 00:05:16.000
And so typically

00:05:16.000 --> 00:05:16.833
what happens

00:05:16.833 --> 00:05:18.358
is that they need to actually

00:05:18.358 --> 00:05:20.080
ligate or cauterize

00:05:20.080 --> 00:05:21.310
some of the vessels,

00:05:21.310 --> 00:05:22.500
blocks some of the vessels

00:05:22.500 --> 00:05:24.200
that are in between the two

00:05:24.200 --> 00:05:27.470
so that it doesn't shut blood
to one twin over the other.

00:05:27.470 --> 00:05:30.130
Because what we usually see
is one twin is thriving.

00:05:30.130 --> 00:05:32.010
One is bigger, one is smaller.

00:05:32.010 --> 00:05:34.050
- Why would they do that for

00:05:34.050 --> 00:05:35.420
RH and compatibility?

00:05:35.420 --> 00:05:37.950
- That's why this is all very confusing

00:05:37.950 --> 00:05:39.330
because usually

00:05:39.330 --> 00:05:41.132
it's not due to RH.

00:05:41.132 --> 00:05:42.610
(both laughing)

00:05:42.610 --> 00:05:46.130
Usually this is just a whole
thing happening on its own.

00:05:46.130 --> 00:05:48.120
So we went from preeclampsia

00:05:48.120 --> 00:05:51.150
to RH incompatibility

00:05:51.150 --> 00:05:53.570
to Twin-Twin Transfusion Syndrome.

00:05:53.570 --> 00:05:54.560
This is very confusing.

00:05:54.560 --> 00:05:55.500
- Okay, interesting.

00:05:55.500 --> 00:05:56.333
Let's see where this goes.

00:05:56.333 --> 00:05:58.030
- One of the amniotic sacs ruptured,

00:05:58.030 --> 00:05:59.237
the strong twins'.

00:06:01.570 --> 00:06:02.463
- Dr. I need help.

00:06:02.463 --> 00:06:03.296
(Dr. Irobunda laughs)

00:06:03.296 --> 00:06:04.129
- I need a console.

00:06:04.129 --> 00:06:05.647
- I used to, I have no idea why

00:06:05.647 --> 00:06:07.393
the amniotic sacs ruptured.

00:06:07.393 --> 00:06:10.370
- By helping the weak, we hurt the strong.

00:06:10.370 --> 00:06:11.210
C-section?

00:06:11.210 --> 00:06:13.830
- If. we do a C-section
to save the strong baby,

00:06:13.830 --> 00:06:16.460
there's a 90% chance
that weak baby will die.

00:06:16.460 --> 00:06:18.680
But if we do an IMU infusion

00:06:18.680 --> 00:06:19.670
to save the week baby,

00:06:19.670 --> 00:06:22.880
there's an 85% chance
that strong baby will die.

00:06:22.880 --> 00:06:25.210
- I definitely would not recommend

00:06:25.210 --> 00:06:26.870
doing an amnioinfusion.

00:06:26.870 --> 00:06:28.420
We wouldn't want to do it in this case

00:06:28.420 --> 00:06:31.470
because it can increase
the risk of infection.

00:06:31.470 --> 00:06:34.640
Basically, if the amniotic
sac already broke,

00:06:34.640 --> 00:06:35.960
that means there's a hole in it.

00:06:35.960 --> 00:06:38.900
And that means that all kinds
of bacteria can come into it.

00:06:38.900 --> 00:06:41.020
So if we put more amniotic fluid

00:06:41.020 --> 00:06:42.790
and we're just creating this like...

00:06:42.790 --> 00:06:44.000
- Breeding ground.
- Yeah.

00:06:44.000 --> 00:06:46.060
- If there's a strong
baby and a weak baby,

00:06:46.060 --> 00:06:47.330
it's pretty clear which one we save.

00:06:47.330 --> 00:06:48.653
- Survival of the fittest.

00:06:51.470 --> 00:06:52.733
- Sorry, it's just,

00:06:54.290 --> 00:06:55.300
it's not really what we're about.

00:06:55.300 --> 00:06:57.770
Is it as a civilization

00:06:57.770 --> 00:07:00.190
aren't the strong morally
obligated to help the weak?

00:07:00.190 --> 00:07:01.250
- This got really deep, man.

00:07:01.250 --> 00:07:02.920
- Is this going to turn political?

00:07:02.920 --> 00:07:04.946
Like what's about to happen?

00:07:04.946 --> 00:07:08.260
- I mean, I see where the,
where the discussion's going,

00:07:08.260 --> 00:07:09.530
we are faced with these types of things

00:07:09.530 --> 00:07:10.440
all the time in medicine,

00:07:10.440 --> 00:07:11.273
where it's like,

00:07:11.273 --> 00:07:13.610
we're picking like the
lesser of two evils,

00:07:13.610 --> 00:07:15.010
where trying to figure out

00:07:15.010 --> 00:07:16.700
what the best mode of action is.

00:07:16.700 --> 00:07:18.190
- What do you do in a situation

00:07:18.190 --> 00:07:19.550
where there's twins like that,

00:07:19.550 --> 00:07:20.840
one is threatened, one is not.

00:07:20.840 --> 00:07:23.170
- It's a group decision.

00:07:23.170 --> 00:07:24.003
And I mean,

00:07:24.003 --> 00:07:27.080
ultimately it's always
the patient, the mom, dad,

00:07:27.080 --> 00:07:29.380
everybody who's involved
decisions about it

00:07:29.380 --> 00:07:31.760
because it's a really big deal.

00:07:31.760 --> 00:07:34.810
And so a lot of times
we have the discussion.

00:07:34.810 --> 00:07:36.720
We give people the options,

00:07:36.720 --> 00:07:39.030
we give them the risk associated with it,

00:07:39.030 --> 00:07:40.090
all the data.

00:07:40.090 --> 00:07:42.080
And then they give us their answer.

00:07:42.080 --> 00:07:44.120
- Delayed interval delivery.

00:07:44.120 --> 00:07:47.080
We induce labor, deliver the strong twin,

00:07:47.080 --> 00:07:49.560
then stop the labor to
keep the weak twin in utero

00:07:49.560 --> 00:07:50.810
until his lungs mature.

00:07:50.810 --> 00:07:52.010
- I don't know anything about this.

00:07:52.010 --> 00:07:53.760
I'm going to ask you something.

00:07:53.760 --> 00:07:55.840
Can you do a C-section,

00:07:55.840 --> 00:07:56.673
get one baby out

00:07:56.673 --> 00:07:57.840
and leave the other baby in?

00:07:57.840 --> 00:07:59.250
- Not for a C-section,

00:07:59.250 --> 00:08:02.620
but I actually saw this
one case in residency

00:08:02.620 --> 00:08:04.220
that was like

00:08:04.220 --> 00:08:06.807
the most crazy thing that I've ever seen.

00:08:06.807 --> 00:08:08.750
And it actually was a set of twins

00:08:08.750 --> 00:08:11.980
where one twin delivered

00:08:11.980 --> 00:08:16.010
and it was maybe like 30 weeks or so.

00:08:16.010 --> 00:08:18.820
And then they did a high ligation,

00:08:18.820 --> 00:08:20.780
the umbilical cord for that one

00:08:20.780 --> 00:08:22.830
and left the second twin in.

00:08:22.830 --> 00:08:25.084
And that one stayed in utero

00:08:25.084 --> 00:08:26.720
- No, don't tell me--
- 35 Weeks.

00:08:26.720 --> 00:08:27.553
- Oh, my god.

00:08:27.553 --> 00:08:28.699
- And then they delivered that one.

00:08:28.699 --> 00:08:29.532
- Wow.

00:08:29.532 --> 00:08:30.560
- It was

00:08:30.560 --> 00:08:33.130
like the craziest slash
coolest thing ever.

00:08:33.130 --> 00:08:34.370
Doctor Thiel!

00:08:34.370 --> 00:08:35.970
I remember you did that (claps)

00:08:35.970 --> 00:08:36.890
- Once she hears

00:08:36.890 --> 00:08:39.400
there's a slightest chance
of saving both babies,

00:08:39.400 --> 00:08:41.510
she won't listen to any alternatives.

00:08:41.510 --> 00:08:44.013
- Statistically, it's a terrible option,

00:08:45.520 --> 00:08:46.910
but it is an option.

00:08:46.910 --> 00:08:47.990
- Present all the options.

00:08:47.990 --> 00:08:48.823
- Yes.

00:08:48.823 --> 00:08:50.580
And see, she has to make the choice.

00:08:50.580 --> 00:08:53.050
- No, you can't seriously
be asking me this.

00:08:53.050 --> 00:08:54.200
- I know that this is something

00:08:54.200 --> 00:08:56.490
no mother should ever have to decide.

00:08:56.490 --> 00:08:57.323
- Good statement.

00:08:57.323 --> 00:08:58.750
What do you do if a patient says,

00:08:58.750 --> 00:09:00.750
what would you do if I
was your family member?

00:09:00.750 --> 00:09:02.940
- I mean, I usually give
them my honest answer,

00:09:02.940 --> 00:09:04.320
but I always say that like

00:09:04.320 --> 00:09:06.500
my family may be different than yours.

00:09:06.500 --> 00:09:08.310
My situation may be different than yours,

00:09:08.310 --> 00:09:09.350
but it's really hard

00:09:09.350 --> 00:09:13.270
because you don't know where
people's actual values stand.

00:09:13.270 --> 00:09:14.580
And you're only seeing them

00:09:14.580 --> 00:09:17.010
in that one particular situation.

00:09:17.010 --> 00:09:18.820
You're seeing them in
that one particular time

00:09:18.820 --> 00:09:19.653
in their lives.

00:09:19.653 --> 00:09:20.486
What about you?

00:09:20.486 --> 00:09:21.880
- I usually give full on

00:09:21.880 --> 00:09:23.720
as if I'm giving my
family's recommendation,

00:09:23.720 --> 00:09:25.060
but then afterwards say like,

00:09:25.060 --> 00:09:26.027
that doesn't mean you should do this.

00:09:26.027 --> 00:09:27.160
(they laugh)

00:09:27.160 --> 00:09:29.160
Don't use that as the
reason why you should do it.

00:09:29.160 --> 00:09:30.500
Unless I really feel like

00:09:30.500 --> 00:09:31.800
for whatever reason,

00:09:31.800 --> 00:09:33.170
they really should be doing this

00:09:33.170 --> 00:09:34.530
or like avoiding something.

00:09:34.530 --> 00:09:35.690
- Exactly, usually...

00:09:35.690 --> 00:09:37.770
Exactly, it depends on the decision.

00:09:37.770 --> 00:09:39.190
Cause like this one is hard.

00:09:39.190 --> 00:09:40.290
- Yeah, it's life or death.

00:09:40.290 --> 00:09:41.842
It's like big deal.
- This is hard.

00:09:41.842 --> 00:09:43.370
- (indistinct) for an amnioinfusion.

00:09:43.370 --> 00:09:45.160
- Have you named them?

00:09:45.160 --> 00:09:46.053
- Dr. Allen?

00:09:50.270 --> 00:09:52.010
- Is that typical?

00:09:52.010 --> 00:09:53.110
People name babies

00:09:54.110 --> 00:09:54.943
in utero?

00:09:54.943 --> 00:09:57.330
- Yeah. People name babies
in utero all the time,

00:09:57.330 --> 00:09:58.337
No.

00:09:58.337 --> 00:10:00.620
No, I can't do this.

00:10:00.620 --> 00:10:01.453
- Oh.

00:10:03.420 --> 00:10:05.600
- You have to see both of them.

00:10:05.600 --> 00:10:07.980
- OB/GYN is so tough sometimes.

00:10:07.980 --> 00:10:10.370
- But, I don't think
these are OB/GYN doctors.

00:10:10.370 --> 00:10:11.778
- They're not.
- They're not.

00:10:11.778 --> 00:10:14.440
- But OB/GYN is hard sometimes.

00:10:14.440 --> 00:10:16.330
The reason why I did OB/GYN

00:10:16.330 --> 00:10:17.980
was very much self serving

00:10:17.980 --> 00:10:20.280
because I was like, I
don't like that outcomes.

00:10:20.280 --> 00:10:21.340
- That's not self-serving,

00:10:21.340 --> 00:10:22.810
that's the opposite of self-serving.

00:10:22.810 --> 00:10:25.160
- Personally, I don't know how I would be

00:10:25.160 --> 00:10:27.360
if I saw like so many,

00:10:27.360 --> 00:10:29.040
like people dealing with really,

00:10:29.040 --> 00:10:30.910
really bad medical problems.

00:10:30.910 --> 00:10:31.800
And so OB

00:10:31.800 --> 00:10:33.670
we deliver babies all the time.

00:10:33.670 --> 00:10:35.800
- Oh, I see what you mean.

00:10:35.800 --> 00:10:37.300
You mean that like you're seeing

00:10:37.300 --> 00:10:39.570
mostly healthy patients.
- Mostly healthy patient.

00:10:39.570 --> 00:10:41.940
- As opposed to like seeing pathologies.

00:10:41.940 --> 00:10:42.773
- Right.

00:10:42.773 --> 00:10:43.630
- Got it. Okay. Makes sense.

00:10:43.630 --> 00:10:45.790
- And then like even GYN side,

00:10:45.790 --> 00:10:46.700
it's usually like,

00:10:46.700 --> 00:10:48.740
oh, you have an itch? I can fix that.

00:10:48.740 --> 00:10:49.813
Oh, you're bleeding too much?

00:10:49.813 --> 00:10:50.646
I can fix that!

00:10:50.646 --> 00:10:52.130
- And you can prevent stuff.

00:10:52.130 --> 00:10:53.970
- Exactly.
- Cervical cancer,

00:10:53.970 --> 00:10:55.340
HPV vaccines.

00:10:55.340 --> 00:10:57.190
- Yeah. HPV vaccines.

00:10:57.190 --> 00:10:58.410
Gardasil, word.

00:10:58.410 --> 00:11:00.093
- Insert a 16 gauge IV.

00:11:01.226 --> 00:11:02.476
- 16 gauge, that's thick.

00:11:03.380 --> 00:11:05.400
- You need big, in OB.

00:11:05.400 --> 00:11:06.520
That's when you need to put that fluid

00:11:06.520 --> 00:11:07.920
and you got to run it fast.

00:11:07.920 --> 00:11:10.580
- And they're inducing a pregnancy here.

00:11:10.580 --> 00:11:11.480
To be delivered.

00:11:11.480 --> 00:11:13.012
- Yeah. And it's twins.

00:11:13.012 --> 00:11:15.679
(woman moaning)

00:11:19.230 --> 00:11:21.170
- His lungs are also under developed.

00:11:21.170 --> 00:11:23.787
He needs to be placed on a ventilator.

00:11:23.787 --> 00:11:24.620
(eerie music)

00:11:24.620 --> 00:11:26.127
- She's having another contraction.

00:11:26.127 --> 00:11:28.710
- (indistinct) Calcium channel blockers.

00:11:28.710 --> 00:11:30.610
- Am I going to lose both of them?

00:11:30.610 --> 00:11:32.360
- I don't know.

00:11:32.360 --> 00:11:33.193
- Good answer.

00:11:33.193 --> 00:11:34.200
- Good answer.

00:11:34.200 --> 00:11:35.033
- Kenzie.

00:11:35.033 --> 00:11:36.897
We need to do a C-section.

00:11:36.897 --> 00:11:39.030
No. No, if he comes out, he'll die.

00:11:39.030 --> 00:11:40.920
- If he doesn't you both will.

00:11:40.920 --> 00:11:41.753
- No!

00:11:41.753 --> 00:11:42.586
- 69 over 40.

00:11:42.586 --> 00:11:43.419
- Why would she die?

00:11:43.419 --> 00:11:44.600
Well, that's a really low blood pressure.

00:11:44.600 --> 00:11:45.433
Why is she...?

00:11:45.433 --> 00:11:47.410
- Well I think, because at this point

00:11:47.410 --> 00:11:48.960
now she's hypovolemic

00:11:48.960 --> 00:11:50.880
she probably lost a lot of blood.

00:11:50.880 --> 00:11:52.570
Her uterus is still distend

00:11:52.570 --> 00:11:54.710
because it has the other baby in there.

00:11:54.710 --> 00:11:55.620
But then,

00:11:55.620 --> 00:11:56.453
you know,

00:11:56.453 --> 00:11:58.100
part of maybe the placenta

00:11:58.100 --> 00:12:00.170
for the first baby is like,

00:12:00.170 --> 00:12:02.250
you know, maybe an issue with that.

00:12:02.250 --> 00:12:03.920
And so she's bleeding.

00:12:03.920 --> 00:12:06.700
If they don't basically get
everything out of the uterus

00:12:06.700 --> 00:12:09.430
and let the uterus kind of
clamp down and stop bleeding,

00:12:09.430 --> 00:12:10.320
they both are not going to do well.

00:12:10.320 --> 00:12:12.760
- You know, I had scary moment one time

00:12:12.760 --> 00:12:14.630
I delivered a baby,

00:12:14.630 --> 00:12:15.760
vaginal delivery,

00:12:15.760 --> 00:12:17.040
it seemed like a totally normal,

00:12:17.040 --> 00:12:18.010
like nothing happened,

00:12:18.010 --> 00:12:19.590
and all of a sudden

00:12:19.590 --> 00:12:21.170
I'm like doing my check

00:12:21.170 --> 00:12:22.936
and I feel like something's not right.

00:12:22.936 --> 00:12:23.769
- Uh-oh!

00:12:23.769 --> 00:12:25.690
- And I didn't know what's going on

00:12:25.690 --> 00:12:26.523
because I was like,

00:12:26.523 --> 00:12:27.850
something just doesn't feel right.

00:12:27.850 --> 00:12:29.360
So I was like, I think
we need to call the OB

00:12:29.360 --> 00:12:30.380
and she says "no, we don't."

00:12:30.380 --> 00:12:31.440
Like, no, we really need to.

00:12:31.440 --> 00:12:32.530
Inverted uterus.

00:12:32.530 --> 00:12:34.090
- Oh, man.

00:12:34.090 --> 00:12:35.179
- And he came in

00:12:35.179 --> 00:12:36.330
- And he go...

00:12:36.330 --> 00:12:37.530
- And it was the most painful,

00:12:37.530 --> 00:12:39.736
most excruciating thing
I've ever witnessed live.

00:12:39.736 --> 00:12:40.630
Have you, have you had that happen?

00:12:40.630 --> 00:12:41.690
- Oh, yeah.
- Yeah?

00:12:41.690 --> 00:12:43.380
Well, I got called into a room once

00:12:43.380 --> 00:12:44.970
to a quick vaginal exam

00:12:44.970 --> 00:12:45.803
and I'm like,

00:12:45.803 --> 00:12:47.400
yup, that would be the endometrium.

00:12:47.400 --> 00:12:49.060
So that's the inner lining of the uterus,

00:12:49.060 --> 00:12:50.410
that I'm staring at.

00:12:50.410 --> 00:12:52.120
As soon as I opened her vagina,

00:12:52.120 --> 00:12:53.920
I tried to do it first

00:12:53.920 --> 00:12:55.380
and it wasn't going in

00:12:55.380 --> 00:12:57.830
because it had contracted a little bit.

00:12:57.830 --> 00:13:00.210
So we had to give her something.

00:13:00.210 --> 00:13:01.510
- To relax her first?

00:13:01.510 --> 00:13:02.647
Because she was probably bleeding a lot.

00:13:02.647 --> 00:13:03.760
- She was bleeding a lot

00:13:03.760 --> 00:13:05.690
and so we had to give
her something to relax it

00:13:05.690 --> 00:13:07.373
and back up.

00:13:08.810 --> 00:13:10.630
- We're doing a C-section,

00:13:10.630 --> 00:13:11.670
let's prep and drape her.

00:13:11.670 --> 00:13:12.737
- No!

00:13:12.737 --> 00:13:13.570
My kid.

00:13:15.163 --> 00:13:16.890
- Whether you're doing it right there,

00:13:16.890 --> 00:13:18.930
I've never seen an in room C-section,

00:13:18.930 --> 00:13:19.970
have you?
- Yeah.

00:13:19.970 --> 00:13:23.130
But usually the patient's not

00:13:23.130 --> 00:13:24.810
with us anymore.

00:13:24.810 --> 00:13:26.790
It usually is post mortem.
- Oh, okay.

00:13:26.790 --> 00:13:28.053
- Try it. Breathe.

00:13:29.527 --> 00:13:30.360
Good.

00:13:34.951 --> 00:13:38.118
They're not going to give her a drape?

00:13:39.790 --> 00:13:41.760
- 100 over 65.

00:13:41.760 --> 00:13:43.259
- Contractions are stopping.

00:13:43.259 --> 00:13:45.960
(peaceful music)

00:13:45.960 --> 00:13:47.820
- I mean, you're not out of the woods yet.

00:13:47.820 --> 00:13:49.100
- No.
- So the peaceful music

00:13:49.100 --> 00:13:50.180
shouldn't start just yet.

00:13:50.180 --> 00:13:51.013
- Not yet.

00:13:51.013 --> 00:13:53.427
- It's 26 week--
- It's super high risk.

00:13:53.427 --> 00:13:54.778
- And the other baby too.

00:13:54.778 --> 00:13:57.361
(gentle music)

00:13:58.220 --> 00:13:59.518
- I, judge this.

00:13:59.518 --> 00:14:01.700
Does this looks like a 26 week preemie?

00:14:01.700 --> 00:14:03.610
- Looks, pretty young.

00:14:03.610 --> 00:14:06.330
Also, usually they don't
bring the baby to you.

00:14:06.330 --> 00:14:07.163
- Yeah.

00:14:07.163 --> 00:14:08.020
You go to the nursery

00:14:08.020 --> 00:14:09.810
or the NICU in this case.

00:14:09.810 --> 00:14:11.030
We got through one episode.

00:14:11.030 --> 00:14:12.030
Yay.

00:14:12.030 --> 00:14:13.810
All right, guys, so
check out more episodes

00:14:13.810 --> 00:14:14.940
of "The Good Doctor" reviews.

